  "body": "Would it be possible to add a clarification of [4.6.7.1 Link type \"alternate\"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#rel-alternate) that a `link`’s `type` of `application/atom+xml;type=entry` (as per [RFC 5023](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5023.html#section-16.6)) is not an autodiscovery link for a feed but represents a true *alternate* representation of the current page as an Atom Entry Document?\r\n\r\nAfter all, it is not inconceivable that a page’s main `<article>` is also available in an alternate, non-feed representation as a single Atom `<entry>`, so this fact should be expressible somehow.\r\n\r\nWhile I think the current wording, which talks about a type rather than a type’s _essence_ of `application/atom+xml` already implies this distinction, it IMHO wouldn’t hurt to stress it in the text or as part of the example:\r\n\r\n> For the purposes of feed autodiscovery, user agents should consider all link elements in the document with the `alternate` keyword used and with their type `attribute` set to the value `application/rss+xml` or the value `application/atom+xml`.",
